\f0\fs24 \cf0 ========================================\
  FLASHING BOARDS \'97 SETUP & HOW TO USE\
========================================\
\
This folder contains everything you need to flash a microcontroller\
without opening any code:\
\
  - flash_microcontroller.command   (the script you run)\
  - a firmware file ending in .hex   (the firmware that gets flashed)\
\
Keep the script and the .hex file(s) together in this folder. The\
script automatically finds the firmware next to itself, so it doesn't\
matter where your Google Drive lives on your Mac.\
\
The .hex file can be named anything (e.g. underfacer_v2.hex) so you\
can keep track of versions over time:\
\
  - If there's ONE .hex file in the folder, the script uses it\
    automatically.\
  - If there are SEVERAL .hex files, the script lists them and asks\
    which one you want to flash.\
\
\
----------------------------------------\
ONE-TIME SETUP (do this once per Mac)\
----------------------------------------\
\
1) INSTALL HOMEBREW, THEN AVRDUDE  (do these two IN ORDER)\
\
   Open the Terminal app first: press Cmd+Space, type "Terminal",\
   hit Enter.\
\
   1a) INSTALL HOMEBREW\
       Homebrew is the installer that avrdude needs. Paste this\
       line, press Enter, and follow the prompts (it may ask for\
       your Mac password and take a few minutes):\
\
           /bin/bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)"\
\
       Wait until it finishes and returns you to a normal prompt\
       before moving on. (If Homebrew is already installed, you can\
       skip straight to 1b.)\
\
   1b) INSTALL AVRDUDE\
       This is the tool that actually talks to the board. Now paste\
       this line and press Enter:\
\
           brew install avrdude\
\
       Note: this step ONLY works after Homebrew (step 1a) has\
       finished installing.\
\
2) MAKE THE SCRIPT RUNNABLE\
   Google Drive removes the "runnable" flag from the file, so turn\
   it back on once. In Terminal, type this exactly (with a trailing\
   space), then DRAG the flash_microcontroller.command file from\
   Finder onto the Terminal window \'97 it fills in the path for you \'97\
   then press Enter:\
\
       chmod +x\
\
   (So the finished line looks like:\
       chmod +x /Users/you/.../flash_microcontroller.command )\
\
\
----------------------------------------\
RUNNING IT\
----------------------------------------\
\
- First time only: RIGHT-CLICK the flash_microcontroller.command\
  file and choose "Open". macOS will warn that it's from an\
  unidentified developer \'97 click "Open" to confirm. After this\
  first time, you can just double-click it normally.\
\
- Plug in your programmer and board.\
\
- Double-click flash_microcontroller.command. A window opens:\
\
    * If the folder has more than one .hex file, it lists them and\
      asks which to flash \'97 type the number and press Enter.\
\
    * Then it asks which chip you're flashing:\
          1) ATtiny44\
          2) ATtiny84\
          3) ATmega328PB\
      Type 1, 2, or 3 and press Enter.\
\
- It flashes the board, then waits. Press Enter to flash the next\
  board, or type Q and press Enter to quit. Great for flashing a\
  batch of the same chip in a row.\
\
\
----------------------------------------\
IF SOMETHING GOES WRONG\
----------------------------------------\
\
"avrdude is not installed"\
   -> Do step 1 above: install Homebrew (1a) FIRST, then run\
      brew install avrdude (1b).\
\
"command not found: brew"\
   -> Homebrew isn't installed yet. Do step 1a first, wait for it\
      to finish, then run brew install avrdude.\
\
"no .hex file found in this folder"\
   -> Make sure the firmware (.hex) file is in the SAME folder as\
      the script. Don't move the script out on its own.\
\
The window flashes open and closes instantly\
   -> The script hasn't been made runnable. Do step 2 above.\
\
"cannot be opened because it is from an unidentified developer"\
   -> Right-click the file and choose "Open" (not double-click)\
      the first time. See "Running it" above.\
\
Board won't flash / avrdude errors\
   -> Check the programmer and board are plugged in properly and\
      you picked the right chip number.\
\
